From c7f5b6308aae7dce4a0e1f943570f2aba7bc9585 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: build <build@localhost>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 15:05:38 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 6/9] sdl1: full screen keeps a 4:3 picture, as on a real Apple
 II monitor

'Full Screen Aspect' in linapple.conf sets the shape the picture keeps in
full screen: 4:3 (default; the 560x384 picture with taller-than-wide
pixels, as a real Apple II monitor or TV showed it), pixel (square
pixels, 560:384), stretch (fill the screen) or any W:H. The rest of the
screen stays black with the picture in the middle.

'Monitor Aspect' is the shape of the monitor's lit area: auto (default)
takes the screen mode's pixels as square; a wide monitor that stretches a
4:3 mode over its panel is named by its shape (16:9, 16:10, ...) so the
picture still looks 4:3. Alt+F6 cycles through the shapes, refits the
picture at once and keeps the choice in linapple.conf.

'Scale Mode': aspect (default) makes the picture as big as the screen
allows; sharp scales its height by a whole number so every Apple line is
the same number of screen lines, keeping the shape.

Started full screen (-f), the picture used to fill the whole screen
whatever its shape; it now gets the same fit as switching to full screen
later.
---
 src/frontends/sdl1/Frame.cpp | 161 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 tests/test_frontend_sdl1.cpp |  97 ++++++++++++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 250 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

